event: final
data: {"type":"final","sequence":0,"request_id":"f67f8f5f-88ef-4d68-b2ce-1f03865c68c6","status":true,"message":"Chat turn processed successfully.","timestamp":"2026-08-14T08:28:56.641723+00:00","data":{"ai_message":"This is a strong opportunity to establish a repeatable brand-health tracker for Epic Games and Fortnite among Indian mobile gamers. The brief below carries forward the approved framework, covering awareness, competitive usage, live-service engagement, loyalty, advocacy, switching, social play, and in-app spending. I have retained a zero-stimulus tracking design because the study measures existing brand and usage behavior rather than testing concepts or advertising assets. Please review the methodology and audience assumptions below and share any changes before we generate the survey.","chat_id":"03a74ac5-bd15-459c-b9fe-f6417495eae6","guest_id":null,"chat_turn_id":"64e757da-6f93-4c8c-8ba5-2e8b4b9a2f86","chat_name":"New Chat (2026-08-14 08:26)","turn_number":2,"survey_turn_number":null,"questions":[],"media_request":null,"research_brief":"# Research Brief: Fortnite & Epic Games Brand Health Among Indian Mobile Gamers\n\n### **Background & Context**\n\nEpic Games and Fortnite operate in a fast-moving mobile gaming environment where player attention can move between Fortnite, BGMI/PUBG, Free Fire, Apex Legends, and other live-service titles. The business trigger is the need to distinguish short-term effects from durable changes in awareness, consideration, usage, loyalty, advocacy, and competitive position across repeated waves.\n\nThe wider games market continues to expand. Newzoo estimates the global games market at $188.8 billion in 2025 and forecasts growth to $206.5 billion by 2028, with 3.6 billion players in 2025. Mobile engagement and monetisation remain strategically important, making platform behavior, session frequency, and mobile spending essential tracker measures. According to Newzoo, mobile games revenue is forecast to reach $121.1 billion in 2026. ([newzoo.com](https://newzoo.com/reports/global-games-market-report), [newzoo.com](https://newzoo.com/articles/global-games-market-2025))\n\nIndia is a high-reach gaming market with substantial growth potential. BITKRAFT and Redseer project India’s digital gaming market to double by FY2030, with battle royale and hybrid-casual titles among the growth areas. Sensor Tower also identifies India as a global leader in mobile-game downloads while highlighting continuing opportunities to improve monetisation. ([bitkraft.vc](https://bitkraft.vc/insights/bitkraft-india-report), [sensortower.com](https://sensortower.com/ja/state-of-mobile-games-in-india-2025-report))\n\nIndia’s monetisation environment makes payer behavior especially important to track. The Economic Times, citing BITKRAFT–Redseer, reports that average revenue per paying user has increased materially and that in-app purchases are expected to become a larger contributor to Indian gaming revenue. Fortnite’s mobile ecosystem also includes Battle Royale, creative experiences, social play, live events, and optional in-app purchases, making loyalty broader than gameplay frequency alone. ([economictimes.indiatimes.com](http://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/media/entertainment/online-gaming-india-krafton-bgmi-rooter-bitkraft-redseer-nazara-in-app-purchases-driving-growth/articleshow/126368318.cms), [play.google.com](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?hl=en-IN&id=com.epicgames.fortnite))\n\n---\n\n### **Research Objectives**\n\nBased on the approved strategic framework, this study will address the following business objectives.\n\n#### **Strategic Objectives**\n\n1. **Measure** the strength of Epic Games and Fortnite’s brand-health funnel among Indian mobile gamers in order to guide brand, acquisition, retention, and competitive strategy.\n2. **Quantify** Fortnite’s position within the Indian battle royale ecosystem in order to identify opportunities for growth, conquest, and win-back.\n3. **Determine** which gameplay, social, creator, collaboration, seasonal, and monetisation experiences strengthen loyalty in order to prioritise marketing and product investment.\n4. **Identify** the behavioral and perceptual drivers of continued usage, advocacy, switching, and return in order to improve retention and reactivation.\n5. **Assess** how in-app purchasing and perceived value contribute to engagement and loyalty in order to inform cosmetic, collaboration, event, and offer strategy.\n\n#### **Tactical Objectives**\n\n1. **Measure** unaided awareness, aided awareness, familiarity, consideration, current usage, recent usage, and preference for Epic Games, Fortnite, and tracked competitors.\n2. **Map** the competitive repertoire of Indian mobile gamers, including concurrent usage of Fortnite, BGMI/PUBG, Free Fire, Apex Legends, and other relevant games.\n3. **Quantify** Fortnite’s share of recent play, frequency of play, session duration, multiplayer participation, and platform mix.\n4. **Identify** the strongest associations linked to Fortnite, including fun, creativity, social connection, variety, competition, cultural relevance, trust, value, and distinctiveness.\n5. **Measure** awareness and participation in seasonal updates, limited-time events, collaborations, creator-made experiences, esports, streaming, and community activity.\n6. **Determine** the triggers and barriers associated with switching, uninstalling, lapsed usage, and returning to Fortnite.\n7. **Assess** the relationship between squad play, friend invitations, creator influence, sharing, streaming, and community participation and stronger loyalty or advocacy.\n8. **Quantify** payer incidence, purchase frequency, recent purchase occasions, broad INR spending bands, purchase categories, and perceived value.\n9. **Identify** barriers to in-app spending, including price, payment friction, low perceived value, lack of interest, parental or household restrictions, and preference for free content.\n10. **Measure** likelihood to continue, recommend, invite friends, return after a break, and choose Fortnite over alternatives using loyalty and advocacy indicators.\n11. **Validate** whether Fortnite’s differentiation is driven more by its wider ecosystem, collaborations, creativity, and social experience than by battle royale gameplay alone.\n12. **Compare** current Fortnite players, lapsed players, competitor-only players, frequent gamers, occasional gamers, payers, and non-payers to identify high-value growth segments.\n\n---\n\n### **Type of Study**\n\nWe will conduct a **Brand Health / Tracking study combined with Usage & Attitude, Competitive Landscape, and selected loyalty and engagement measures**. This is the right study type because the business needs a stable wave-to-wave view of brand salience, awareness, consideration, usage, perception, loyalty, advocacy, competitive movement, and monetisation behavior.\n\nThe study contains **zero discrete stimuli**. Respondents will not evaluate advertisements, concepts, packs, price cells, or campaign assets. The emphasis is on existing brands, real-world gaming behavior, perceptions, experiences, and movement between titles.\n\n---\n\n### **Survey Design Approach** (Marketing Research Methodology)\n\nThe selected approach is a **linear structured questionnaire using a modular funnel and tracking-wave design**. It fits this objective because the study must measure natural behavior before brand evaluation, establish a consistent core across waves, and explain why Fortnite’s brand-health scores change over time. The sequence will follow category behavior, unaided awareness, aided awareness, usage, perceptions, engagement, monetisation, loyalty, and advocacy.\n\nThe stable core will use identical wording and scales across waves for trend KPIs. A small rotating module may be added for current campaigns, collaborations, seasonal updates, creator initiatives, esports moments, monetisation programs, or emerging competitors. Platform targeting will enforce age, gender, city, and NCCS quotas before the survey begins; these variables will not be re-collected in the questionnaire.\n\n**Design Selection:** The selected design is a **zero-stimulus modular tracking design**. Since there are no discrete stimuli to compare, monadic, sequential monadic, paired comparison, A/B, and split-cell designs are not appropriate.\n\n**Alternatives considered:**\n- **Standalone U&A study:** Not chosen because it would provide a behavioral snapshot but would not support reliable wave-over-wave brand-health tracking.\n- **Concept or campaign test:** Not chosen because no specific creative, collaboration asset, product concept, or advertisement is being evaluated.\n- **Segmentation-first study:** Not chosen as the primary design because the immediate business need is tracking; a later segmentation analysis can use the behavioral and attitudinal battery collected here.\n\n**Routing and controls:** Current Fortnite players, lapsed Fortnite players, competitor players, payers, non-payers, and respondents with creator or community engagement will receive relevant follow-up modules. The survey will use skip logic, branching, display logic, termination logic, filter logic, and completion logic. Attention checks will be embedded at approximately a 1:9 ratio. Speeder detection, straight-line detection, open-end gibberish screening, and implausible-behavior checks will be applied.\n\nYou can request a different methodology before generating the survey.\n\n---\n\n### **Question Flow & Survey Phases**\n\n*   **Phase 1 — Warm-Up & Screening:** Confirm recent mobile gaming activity, gaming frequency, platform use, and familiarity with at least one tracked title. This establishes eligibility without beginning with brand judgments.\n*   **Phase 2 — Category Usage & Competitive Context:** Measure genres played, session patterns, multiplayer behavior, game repertoire, time of day, recent switching, uninstalling, and returning. This provides the behavioral context for brand analysis.\n*   **Phase 3 — Unaided Brand Health:** Capture spontaneous awareness of gaming companies, games, battle royale titles, and associations before showing or naming detailed brand lists.\n*   **Phase 4 — Aided Funnel & Competitive Benchmarking:** Measure aided awareness, familiarity, consideration, current usage, recent usage, preference, perceived differentiation, and imagery for Epic Games, Fortnite, and relevant competitors.\n*   **Phase 5 — Fortnite Engagement Deep Dive:** Among relevant respondents, assess play frequency, session duration, squad behavior, social interaction, seasonal updates, collaborations, creator experiences, community activity, esports, streaming, and sharing.\n*   **Phase 6 — Switching, Churn & Return:** Identify reasons for switching, stopping, uninstalling, reducing usage, returning, or choosing a competitor. This phase will support retention, win-back, and conquest decisions.\n*   **Phase 7 — Monetisation & Value:** Measure payer status, purchase frequency, broad INR spend bands, purchase occasions, purchase categories, perceived value, and barriers to in-app spending.\n*   **Phase 8 — Loyalty, Advocacy & Open Ends:** Measure likelihood to continue, recommend, invite friends, return after a break, and choose Fortnite over alternatives. Short open-ended probes will capture the strongest reason to play, the biggest frustration, and the change most likely to increase engagement.\n*   **Phase 9 — Rotating Wave Module:** Add a limited wave-specific module for a current collaboration, seasonal update, creator initiative, campaign, esports moment, or competitive development while keeping the tracking core unchanged.\n\nThe expected instrument is approximately **18–25 questions** and **8–12 minutes**, depending on routing. Break screens may be used between major modules if field testing indicates elevated fatigue.\n\n---\n\n### **Key Data Points to Measure**\n\n#### **Primary Metrics (The KPIs)**\n\n- **Unaided awareness** - enables top-of-mind salience benchmarking for Epic Games, Fortnite, and battle royale competitors.\n- **Aided awareness** - measures total brand reach after prompted recognition.\n- **Familiarity** - distinguishes superficial awareness from meaningful brand knowledge.\n- **Consideration** - identifies brands entering the active choice set.\n- **Current and recent usage** - measures present and recent competitive participation.\n- **Fortnite share of recent play** - estimates Fortnite’s relative role within each respondent’s gaming repertoire.\n- **Usage frequency and session duration** - identifies heavy, moderate, occasional, and lapsed player segments.\n- **Competitive repertoire** - shows which titles are used alongside Fortnite and which competitors represent the greatest substitution risk.\n- **Switch-in and switch-out rates** - identifies conquest and churn movement across waves.\n- **Brand association scores** - measures perceptions of fun, creativity, social connection, variety, competition, cultural relevance, trust, value, and distinctiveness.\n- **Differentiation index** - quantifies the attributes Fortnite owns more strongly than competitors.\n- **Likelihood to continue** - provides a forward-looking retention indicator.\n- **Recommendation likelihood and NPS** - measures advocacy potential using the standard 0–10 likelihood-to-recommend framework with reason probing.\n- **Likelihood to invite friends** - measures social acquisition potential.\n- **Likelihood to return after a break** - identifies reactivation potential among lapsed players.\n- **Likelihood to choose Fortnite over alternatives** - measures competitive preference and choice strength.\n- **Payer incidence** - quantifies the percentage of respondents making in-app purchases.\n- **Recent purchase incidence and purchase frequency** - tracks monetisation participation over a defined recent period.\n- **Broad INR spend bands** - supports payer segmentation without requiring sensitive exact-spend reporting.\n- **Perceived value of cosmetics, events, collaborations, and other content** - connects spending to value perception.\n\n#### **Supporting Metrics (Contextual Data)**\n\n- Mobile gaming frequency and recency - provides a basis for qualifying active, occasional, and lapsed gamers.\n- Platform mix across mobile, PC, and console - shows cross-platform behavior and the role of mobile within the wider gaming journey.\n- Game genres played - identifies adjacent category interests and addressable audiences.\n- Multiplayer and squad participation - explains the social foundation of retention.\n- Voice, text, friend-invite, sharing, and streaming behavior - measures community and advocacy mechanisms.\n- Seasonal-update awareness and participation - evaluates the role of live-service content.\n- Collaboration awareness and participation - identifies partnership-driven engagement.\n- Creator-made experience usage - measures the contribution of user-generated and creator-led content.\n- Esports and competitive-content engagement - identifies the role of competitive culture in brand strength.\n- Reasons for stopping, switching, uninstalling, and returning - diagnoses churn and win-back triggers.\n- Reasons for not paying - identifies price, value, trust, payment, and permission barriers.\n- Segment status - supports comparison of current players, lapsed players, competitor-only players, payers, non-payers, and high-frequency gamers.\n- Field period and wave identifier - enables interpretation of changes alongside major seasons, launches, partnerships, and market events.\n\n#### **Advanced Analytics We'll Apply**\n\n- **Cross-tabulation and significance testing** to compare waves, cities, age brackets, gender, NCCS classes, usage frequency, payer status, and competitive segments.\n- **Brand-funnel conversion analysis** to identify where Fortnite gains or loses respondents from awareness through loyalty.\n- **Competitive gap analysis** to compare Fortnite with BGMI/PUBG, Free Fire, Apex Legends, and other tracked titles.\n- **Driver analysis** to estimate which perceptions and behaviors are most strongly associated with loyalty, advocacy, and continued usage.\n- **Switching-flow analysis** to map movement into and out of Fortnite across waves.\n- **Segment profiling** to identify high-value players, growth prospects, lapsed users, competitor-only users, payers, and non-payers.\n- **Semantic-differential and association analysis** to evaluate Fortnite’s perceptual territory relative to competitors.\n- **Trend decomposition** to distinguish durable movement from effects associated with season launches, collaborations, campaigns, or major gaming events.\n- **Open-end thematic and sentiment analysis** to identify recurring frustrations, motivations, return triggers, and unmet needs.\n\n---\n\n### **Expected Insights & Outcomes**\n\nBy the end of this study, you will possess the data to:\n\n- **Validate:** Whether Fortnite has stronger spontaneous awareness than most competing battle royale titles among active Indian mobile gamers.\n- **Validate:** Whether Fortnite’s differentiation is driven more by its ecosystem, collaborations, creativity, and social experience than by battle royale gameplay alone.\n- **Validate:** Whether frequent multiplayer and community participation are associated with stronger loyalty and advocacy.\n- **Validate:** Whether seasonal updates and collaborations increase engagement, and whether their effect is stronger among existing players than among lapsed or competitor-only players.\n- **Validate:** Whether spending is influenced by perceived cosmetic and event value rather than by play frequency alone.\n- **Validate:** Whether lapsed players are more likely to return after a major update, collaboration, friend invitation, or new content experience.\n\n- **Decide:** Which Indian gamer segments should receive priority in marketing and campaign planning.\n- **Decide:** Which partnerships, collaborations, creator programs, and community initiatives are most aligned with the strongest engagement drivers.\n- **Decide:** Whether Fortnite’s positioning should emphasise social play, creativity, competition, cultural relevance, live events, variety, or another differentiated strength.\n- **Decide:** Where retention, win-back, and competitive-conquest activity should be focused.\n- **Decide:** How payer and non-payer strategies should differ based on perceived value, purchase behavior, and spending barriers.\n- **Decide:** Which engagement and monetisation signals should be used as leading indicators in future tracking waves.\n\n- **Strategize:** Brand communication, partnership, collaboration, creator, community, retention, win-back, monetisation, and competitive-conquest priorities using evidence from behavioral and perceptual movement.\n\n**Key hypotheses being tested:**\n\n1. Fortnite has stronger spontaneous awareness than most competing battle royale titles among active Indian mobile gamers.\n2. Fortnite’s differentiation is driven more by its ecosystem, collaborations, creativity, and social experience than by battle royale gameplay alone.\n3. Frequent multiplayer and community participation are associated with stronger loyalty and advocacy.\n4. Seasonal updates and collaborations increase engagement, with stronger effects among existing players than among lapsed or competitor-only players.\n5. Spending is influenced by perceived cosmetic and event value, not only by play frequency.\n6. Lapsed players are more likely to return after a major update, collaboration, friend invitation, or new content experience.\n\n**KPIs to report:** Unaided awareness; aided awareness; familiarity; consideration; current usage; recent usage; usage frequency; session duration; competitive repertoire; Fortnite share of recent play; switch-in and switch-out rates; key association scores; differentiation index; likelihood to continue; NPS and recommendation likelihood; likelihood to invite friends; likelihood to return; likelihood to choose Fortnite over alternatives; multiplayer participation; seasonal-update engagement; collaboration engagement; creator-experience engagement; community participation; payer incidence; recent purchase incidence; purchase frequency; broad INR spend bands; value perception; barriers to spending; and top reasons for switching, returning, stopping, or purchasing.\n\n---\n\n### **Methodology** [See why](hercules://explain?topic=Methodology)\n\nWe will deploy an **approximately 22-question, 8–12-minute** tracking survey on the **SuperJ mobile application and the web** to **450** verified Indian respondents for the baseline wave.\n\nThe questionnaire will use a zero-stimulus modular tracking design. The core order will be category behavior, unaided awareness, aided awareness, brand funnel, competitive benchmarking, Fortnite engagement, switching and churn, monetisation, loyalty, advocacy, and open-ended feedback. A rotating wave module may be added without changing the core KPI wording.\n\nA total sample of 450 provides a practical directional baseline for overall tracking and enables comparison across major behavioral segments. Results will be interpreted using confidence intervals, significance testing, and segment base sizes rather than treating small subgroup differences as definitive. Future waves should preserve the core sample structure and field timing wherever possible.\n\nThe question mix will use behavioral frequency measures, fully labeled five-point scales, semantic-differential brand imagery measures, NPS, forced-choice comparisons where useful, and short open-ended probes at high-value diagnostic points. Long agree/disagree grids will be avoided to reduce acquiescence and positive-end response bias.\n\n**Data Collection Approach:** SuperJ mobile application and web panel, with audience targeting enforced before survey start.\n\n**Quality Controls:** Attention checks, speedster removal, straight-liner checks, open-end gibberish filtering, implausible gaming-behavior checks, duplicate screening, and routing validation.\n\n---\n\n### **Target Audience** [See why](hercules://explain?topic=Target+Audience)\n\nThe study will survey **Indian mobile gamers spanning frequent, occasional, and lapsed players; current Fortnite players; players of competing battle royale titles; and relevant household decision-makers for younger players where platform policy permits**. Because the platform serves respondents aged 18 and above, the sample will include adults who can report their own gaming behavior and spending decisions.\n\n**Total Sample Size:** 450 respondents\n\n**Geographic Focus:** Delhi, Mumbai, Bangalore, Hyderabad, and Pune. These cities provide a focused metro sample for the baseline wave while avoiding an overly dispersed city design. The five city quotas sum to the full sample.\n\n**Demographics:**\n\n- **Gender:** 50% male and 50% female, represented as 225 male and 225 female respondents.\n- **Age Range:** 18-24, 24-35, and 35-45. Quotas will be 180 respondents aged 18-24, 170 aged 24-35, and 100 aged 35-45.\n- **Socioeconomic Class:** NCCS A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3, and C1. Analysis will compare higher purchasing-power and broader mass-market segments without using Western income bands.\n\n**Behavioral/Psychographic Filters:**\n\n- Recent mobile gaming activity within the defined qualification period.\n- Current, recent, or lapsed experience with Fortnite or at least one tracked competitor.\n- Coverage of frequent, occasional, and lapsed mobile gamers.\n- Coverage of current Fortnite players, competitor-only players, and former Fortnite players.\n- Analysis by payer versus non-payer status.\n- Analysis by multiplayer participation, creator influence, community participation, seasonal engagement, and collaboration awareness.\n\n**Screening Criteria:**\n\n- Must be an Indian resident aged 18 or above.\n- Must have played mobile games recently or have qualifying recent gaming experience for the lapsed-player route.\n- Must be able to identify at least one tracked gaming title or brand.\n- Must meet the relevant current, recent, or lapsed usage threshold for the route they enter.\n- Must pass platform attention, speed, consistency, and open-end quality checks.\n\n**Statistical justification:** The audience payload mirrors the research brief exactly. Gender, age, and city are independent quota dimensions, and each dimension sums to the total sample of 450 respondents.","research_framework":null,"context_request":null,"qna_context":null,"audience_payload":{"title":"Fortnite India: The Ultimate Player Pulse","created_by":"hercules","total":450,"male":225,"female":225,"Nccs":{"A1":10,"A2":15,"A3":15,"B1":20,"B2":20,"B3":15,"C1":5},"Attributes":{"Frequent mobile gamers":40,"Occasional mobile gamers":25,"Lapsed mobile gamers":15,"Current Fortnite players":35,"Competitor battle royale players":30,"In-app purchase payers":25,"Non-payers":35},"checkCity":true,"18-24":{"total":180},"24-35":{"total":170},"35-45":{"total":100},"Delhi":{"total":90,"tier":"tier1"},"Mumbai":{"total":90,"tier":"tier1"},"Bangalore":{"total":90,"tier":"tier1"},"Hyderabad":{"total":90,"tier":"tier1"},"Pune":{"total":90,"tier":"tier1"},"previousstate":{}},"cta_buttons":["Yes, create the survey."],"end_message":"Please review the selected tracking methodology, city coverage, age structure, and behavioral audience filters. If the framework looks right, we can move forward to generate the survey instrument. You can still request changes to the sample, audience, wave structure, or methodology before launch.","see_why":{"methodology":"The **brand-health tracking methodology** is appropriate because the goal is to measure Fortnite and Epic Games’ awareness, consideration, usage, loyalty, competitive position, and monetisation over repeated waves—not to test a specific concept or campaign. A structured, zero-stimulus questionnaire with a stable KPI core supports reliable trend analysis, while modular routing captures differences among current, lapsed, competitor-only, payer, and community-engaged gamers.","target_audience":"The target audience focuses on **Indian adult mobile gamers across five priority metros**, including current Fortnite players, lapsed users, competitor players, frequent and occasional gamers, payers, and non-payers. This audience directly reflects the strategic need to assess Fortnite’s competitive position, growth and win-back opportunities, engagement drivers, and monetisation potential within India’s high-reach but still-developing mobile gaming market."},"superJSurveyId":"PENDING_8c61b0f1-8c66-4eab-a18b-86e580d819ef","superJSurveyName":"New Chat (2026-08-14 08:26)","businessId":"6a7ed0f015203e92a622fd54","error":null,"changes_detected":true,"log_message":null,"exhausted":false,"usage_info":{"tier_type":"FREE","daily_limit":null,"tokens_used_today":0,"tokens_remaining":0,"chat_context_limit":400000,"chat_tokens_used":0,"chat_tokens_remaining":400000,"monthly_chat_limit":10,"chats_created_this_month":1,"chats_remaining":9,"exhausted":false,"exhausted_reason":null},"business_flow":null,"routing_logic":null,"route_logs":[],"urls_referred":[]},"require_auth":false}

